            'abstract' => 'The double-ψ β-barrel (DPBB) is an ancient protein fold conserved in fundamental molecular machines, including RNA polymerase and AMP phosphorylase [1]. Our previous work demonstrated that the DPBB architecture can be reverse-engineered and rationally redesigned, with its core fold encoded by only seven amino acid types [2]. Though it exhibits remarkable thermostability in modern hyperthermophilic enzymes, how this primordial and structurally simple architecture achieves remarkable thermostability in modern proteins remains poorly understood. We present a comprehensive computational framework integrating evolutionary analysis, comparative structural modeling, multiscale molecular simulations, energetic profiling, free-energy landscape analysis, and rational protein redesign to identify the molecular determinants of DPBB stability. Using DPBB-containing AMP phosphorylases from three phylogenetically diverse <i>Thermococcus</i> species—<i>T. kodakarensis</i>, <i>T. gammatolerans</i>, and <i>T. chitonophagus</i>—we performed 18 μs of molecular simulations across 300–373 K. It was found that the enhanced thermostability was associated with reduced conformational fluctuations, greater secondary-structure preservation, strengthened hydrogen-bond and salt-bridge networks, and increased structural compactness under thermal stress. Free-energy landscapes revealed distinct stability regimes, with <i>T. kodakarensis</i> exhibiting deeper energy minima and reduced conformational heterogeneity. Comparative mutational analysis identified key stability hotspots, several of which were transferable across evolutionary relatives [3]. These findings link the evolutionary simplicity of an ancient fold to conserved molecular principles underlying modern protein thermostability and provide a framework for rationally engineering robust proteins and biocatalysts.',
