            'abstract' => 'Before a model learns anything, we have already shaped what it can see. The same object can be written down in more than one way, and the model only ever meets the version we pick. A protein can be a string of letters, a shape in space or a web of contacts. A medical scan can be raw pixels or a handful of measurements. A market can be a column of prices or a network of traders. Each choice lets the model see some things and hides others. The choice is usually made early and without much thought.

In this talk, we explore what happens when that choice is not given enough thought. A model can get the right answer without understanding the problem. A chest X-ray classifier may learn the markings a hospital puts on its scans and not the disease. Two datasets of the same size can need very different amounts of training data, and the shape of the data often tells us why. Quantum computers add a new twist. Data must be written into qubits before any learning begins, and that single step can decide much of what follows. The same lesson runs through all of these. What the model sees matters as much as the model itself.',
