            'abstract' => 'Innate immune defense against viral pathogens relies on the precise orchestration of transcriptional programs that produce Type I interferons, primarily interferon-beta (<i>IFN-β</i>). Host cells mobilize two central transcription factors—NF-κB and IRF3—to drive this defense, yet how cells coordinate these distinct signaling cascades to achieve context-dependent temporal control over <i>IFN-β</i> remains an open question in dynamical systems biology. Here, we present a probability-based mathematical model of the <i>IFN-β</i> promoter to resolve the combinatorial and temporal logic of NF-κB and IRF3 coregulation. Rather than acting as a static AND-gate, our model reveals a dynamic biphasic handoff driven by temporal bias in transcription factor recruitment. Although both factors possess the structural potential to bind, their kinetic profiles partition the transcriptional output: NF-κB dominates the initial phase to deliver an immediate, elevated burst of <i>IFN-β</i>,after which IRF3 takes charge to orchestrate a delayed but persistent antiviral defense. Furthermore, parametric analysis of the promoter state space predicts that the binding affinity of NF-κB to the <i>IFN-β</i> regulatory elements must be equal to or greater than that of IRF3 to ensure this optimal combination of acute-phase responsiveness and sustained immunity.These findings elucidate why evolution favored coregulation by two independent pathways, establishing that temporal bias and kinetic division of labor provide an effective strategy for filtering cellular stress while sustaining robust antiviral protection.',
