            'abstract' => 'Sustainable agriculture increasingly depends on harnessing the plant microbiome to improve nutrient use, stress resilience and yield while reducing chemical inputs. Yet it remains unclear which microbes matter, when they matter, and at what resolution they must be identified. Our studies address these questions by involving large scale analysis of plant-microbiome data (16S amplicon, metagenome and metatranscriptome) from different plant species across the globe and identified marker bacteria specific to vegetative, reproductive and maturation stages, including <i>Lysobacter</i> <i>soli</i>, <i>Stenotrophomonas</i> <i>rhizophila</i>, and <i>Pseudoduganella</i> <i>guangdongensis</i>. These bacteria were positively associated with nodule density, root length and flowering time, and their expressed genes pointed to roles in nitrogen and cobalt metabolism, biotin metabolism and carbon fixation. Furthermore, we did benchmarking of seven reference-based strain-level tools on simulated and real plant metagenome data as closely related strains of same bacterial species may be beneficial or pathogenic. We found that StrainScan and StrainGE performed best on real data, but accuracy fell as strain diversity rose and no single tool excelled across all conditions, highlighting the need for scalable methods for complex plant microbiomes. Together, these findings provide a roadmap for designing stage-aware, strain-resolved microbial inoculants for sustainable crop production, and show that microbiome-based agriculture requires pairing community-level discovery with strain-level precision.',
