            'abstract' => 'Biological data infrastructures are undergoing a fundamental transformation, driven by unprecedented advances in high-throughput sequencing, multi-omics technologies, artificial intelligence, and the growing recognition of biological data as a strategic resource. What began as relatively simple repositories for storing and sharing nucleotide sequences has evolved into a complex ecosystem of interconnected data resources, computational platforms, and knowledge-generation systems. This evolution raises important questions about how biological data infrastructures should be designed, governed, and sustained to serve both national priorities and global scientific advancement.

Drawing upon the experience of conceptualizing and establishing the Indian Biological Data Centre (IBDC), India\'s first national repository for life science data, this lecture will trace the motivations, challenges, and emerging trends shaping biological data infrastructures. The establishment of IBDC was motivated not merely by the need to create national data storage capacity, but by a broader vision of developing indigenous capabilities for biological data stewardship, enabling equitable access, ensuring interoperability with global resources, and supporting knowledge generation within India.

It is important examine how the convergence of large-scale biological datasets, AI-driven discovery, foundation models, and increasingly complex data governance requirements is redefining the role of biological data centres. These drivers have given rise to the concept of "Twin Sovereignties"—biodata sovereignty and algorithmic sovereignty. While the former concerns the ability to govern biological data, the latter emphasizes the computational capabilities, algorithms, and expertise required to transform these data into scientific knowledge and societal benefits. Data sovereignty without corresponding knowledge-generation capabilities risks creating isolated data silos rather than enabling scientific autonomy.

Against this backdrop, it is important to discuss the need for governance frameworks that balance national interests with international scientific cooperation, ensuring responsible access, interoperability, transparency, and equitable benefit sharing. Finally, one needs to understand the emerging role of India in shaping the next generation of global biological data infrastructures. With its extraordinary biodiversity, expanding genomic initiatives, scientific talent, and growing computational capabilities, India has an opportunity to move beyond being a contributor of biological data to becoming an architect of interoperable, equitable, and knowledge-driven global biodata ecosystems. Indeed, the future of biological data infrastructures will be determined not simply by how much data nations can store, but by how effectively they can govern, interpret, and transform those data into knowledge for the collective benefit of humanity.',
