            'abstract' => 'How much can a cell’s gene expression tell us about its age? Answering this question requires distinguishing age-associated signals from the biological heterogeneity and technical noise inherent in single-cell measurements. In this talk, I will present GERAS (GEnetic Reference for Age of Single-cell), a neural network framework that assigns individual cells to chronological stages using their transcriptomes. Applied to zebrafish beta-cells and human pancreatic cells, GERAS achieved greater than 90% classification accuracy, with validation using independently generated datasets.

Beyond age classification, GERAS provides a framework for investigating how nutritional and metabolic conditions influence cellular aging. Higher calorie intake shifted zebrafish beta-cells towards older transcriptional states, while exploratory analysis of human pancreatic cells suggested an association between higher BMI and older predicted states. Classification also revealed molecular differences between cells with distinct predicted ages, identifying the transcription factor junba as a candidate regulator. Functional experiments demonstrated its role in maintaining juvenile beta-cell proliferation. Together, these findings illustrate how machine learning can connect single-cell heterogeneity with experimentally testable biological mechanisms. I will discuss the opportunities and challenges in extending this approach towards understanding biological age and metabolic disease.',
