Across in-house and multicentric longitudinal aging cohorts, we demonstrate that periventricular (PVWMH) and deep white matter hyperintensities (DWMH) represent biologically distinct manifestations of cerebral small-vessel disease. PVWMH burden is associated with selective structural vulnerability, white-matter disintegrity and cognitive dysfunction. Functionally, PVWMH progressively disrupts the canonical antagonistic organization of the Default Mode Network (DMN) and Dorsal Attention Network (DAN), reducing network segregation and destabilizing intrinsic dynamics beyond normative aging. Molecular profiling links this reorganization to vascular remodeling, blood-brain barrier dysfunction and neuroinflammation, providing a mechanistic bridge from microvascular injury to large-scale circuit dysfunction. Related network alterations in epilepsy suggest convergent systems-level responses across brain disorders.

We further integrate high-resolution hippocampal subfield mapping to characterize regional vulnerability and <i>in vivo</i> 1H-MRS to quantify cerebral metabolic pools, capturing neurochemical phenotypes invisible to conventional imaging.

